            <ArticleTitle>Amyand's hernia with a periappendicular abscess.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1136/bcr-2013-203062</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">bcr2013203062</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We present a rare case of a perforated vermiform appendix presenting as a strangulated inguinal hernia. An 89-year-old man presented to the surgical assessment unit with a 1-week history of progressively worsening abdominal pain, fever and a tender mass in the right iliac fossa. A diagnosis of strangulated inguinal hernia was made. Intraoperatively, a perforated appendix and a pus-filled sac were found. An appendicectomy and a Bassini repair of the hernia were performed with a satisfactory postoperative outcome. In the majority of cases Amyand's hernia is an intraoperative finding and its management depends on the extent of appediceal disease. In cases of perforated appendix with periappendiceal abscess within the hernial sac, an appedicectomy with Bassini's repair is recommended.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
